      "source": [
        "#| echo: false\n",
        "plot_labels = shown_counts[\"Label\"].str.replace(\"MGTA \", \"\", regex=False)\n",
        "plt.barh(plot_labels, shown_counts[\"Times shown\"])\n",
        "plt.title(\"Item Exposure Counts\")\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"Times shown\")\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "00f4046c",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The exposure counts are fairly even, which is reassuring. Balanced exposure matters because it makes the simple counts analysis more interpretable and reduces the risk that some items look popular merely because they were shown more often.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Counts Analysis\n",
        "\n",
        "A simple way to summarize MaxDiff data is to compare how often each item is chosen as best versus worst, conditional on being shown. For item $j$, define\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{score}_j = \\%\\text{best}_j - \\%\\text{worst}_j.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A score near +1 means an item is almost always picked best when shown. A score near -1 means it is almost always picked worst. A score near 0 means the item sits near the middle of the preference ranking."
      ],
      "id": "7b8508e5"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "shown = df.groupby(\"ItemID\").size()\n",
        "best = df[df[\"Response\"] == 1].groupby(\"ItemID\").size()\n",
        "worst = df[df[\"Response\"] == -1].groupby(\"ItemID\").size()\n",
        "\n",
        "counts = (\n",
        "    pd.DataFrame({\"times_shown\": shown, \"times_best\": best, \"times_worst\": worst})\n",
        "      .fillna(0)\n",
        "      .reset_index()\n",
        "      .merge(labels, on=\"ItemID\", how=\"left\")\n",
        ")\n",
        "counts[\"pct_best\"] = counts[\"times_best\"] / counts[\"times_shown\"]\n",
        "counts[\"pct_worst\"] = counts[\"times_worst\"] / counts[\"times_shown\"]\n",
        "counts[\"score\"] = counts[\"pct_best\"] - counts[\"pct_worst\"]\n",
        "\n",
        "resp_ids = df[\"Record ID\"].unique()\n",
        "B = 1000\n",
        "boot_scores = np.zeros((B, len(counts)))\n",
        "\n",
        "for b in range(B):\n",
        "    samp = np.random.choice(resp_ids, size=len(resp_ids), replace=True)\n",
        "    sampled_ids = pd.Series(samp, name=\"Record ID\").to_frame()\n",
        "    boot_df = sampled_ids.merge(df, on=\"Record ID\", how=\"left\")\n",
        "\n",
        "    sh = boot_df.groupby(\"ItemID\").size()\n",
        "    be = boot_df[boot_df[\"Response\"] == 1].groupby(\"ItemID\").size()\n",
        "    wo = boot_df[boot_df[\"Response\"] == -1].groupby(\"ItemID\").size()\n",
        "\n",
        "    tmp = pd.DataFrame({\"shown\": sh, \"best\": be, \"worst\": wo}).fillna(0)\n",
        "    tmp = tmp.reindex(counts[\"ItemID\"]).fillna(0)\n",
        "\n",
        "    boot_scores[b, :] = tmp[\"best\"] / tmp[\"shown\"] - tmp[\"worst\"] / tmp[\"shown\"]\n",
        "\n",
        "counts[[\"ci_low\", \"ci_high\"]] = np.percentile(boot_scores, [2.5, 97.5], axis=0).T\n",
        "counts = counts.sort_values(\"score\", ascending=False).reset_index(drop=True)\n",
        "counts[\"rank\"] = np.arange(1, len(counts) + 1)\n",
        "\n",
        "counts_rounded = counts.copy()\n",
        "for col in [\"pct_best\", \"pct_worst\", \"score\", \"ci_low\", \"ci_high\"]:\n",
        "    counts_rounded[col] = counts_rounded[col].round(3)\n",
        "\n",
        "counts_rounded[[\"rank\", \"Label\", \"times_shown\", \"times_best\", \"times_worst\", \"pct_best\", \"pct_worst\", \"score\", \"ci_low\", \"ci_high\"]]"
      ],
      "id": "4c74d440",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: false\n",
        "plot_counts = counts.sort_values(\"score\", ascending=True)\n",
        "plt.barh(plot_counts[\"Label\"].str.replace(\"MGTA \", \"\", regex=False), plot_counts[\"score\"])\n",
        "plt.errorbar(\n",
        "    plot_counts[\"score\"],\n",
        "    np.arange(len(plot_counts)),\n",
        "    xerr=[plot_counts[\"score\"] - plot_counts[\"ci_low\"], plot_counts[\"ci_high\"] - plot_counts[\"score\"]],\n",
        "    fmt=\"none\",\n",
        "    capsize=3\n",
        ")\n",
        "plt.title(\"Counts Analysis: % Best Minus % Worst\")\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"Counts score\")\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "4dd859f1",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The counts analysis provides a very intuitive first look. The highest-scoring classes are the ones most often chosen as best when they appear, and the lowest-scoring classes are the ones most often chosen as worst. This method is fast and transparent, but it does not explicitly model the full choice process. It uses only the best and worst tallies and ignores the fact that every not-chosen item still contributed information by being available and not selected.\n",
        "\n",
        "One advantage of the bootstrap intervals is that they make the descriptive ranking feel less brittle. If two classes have nearly identical counts scores and their intervals overlap substantially, I should avoid over-interpreting tiny rank differences. The counts analysis is therefore best understood as a descriptive first pass rather than a definitive model of latent utility."
      ],
      "id": "7411c288"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "counts_summary = pd.concat([\n",
        "    counts_rounded[[\"Label\", \"score\"]].head(3).assign(group=\"Top 3 by counts\"),\n",
        "    counts_rounded[[\"Label\", \"score\"]].tail(3).assign(group=\"Bottom 3 by counts\")\n",
        "])\n",
        "counts_summary"
      ],
      "id": "e1ac1c42",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## From MaxDiff Data to MNL Choices\n",
        "\n",
        "The multinomial logit model treats each MaxDiff task as a choice problem. Suppose a task shows items $\\{a,b,c,d\\}$ and the respondent chooses item $b$ as best. The MNL probability of that best choice is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(\\text{best}=b) = \\frac{\\exp(\\beta_b)}{\\exp(\\beta_a)+\\exp(\\beta_b)+\\exp(\\beta_c)+\\exp(\\beta_d)}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Once the best item is removed, the worst pick can be modeled as a second MNL choice among the remaining three items with utilities flipped:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(\\text{worst}=c \\mid \\text{best}=b) = \\frac{\\exp(-\\beta_c)}{\\sum_{j \\in \\{a,c,d\\}} \\exp(-\\beta_j)}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "So each MaxDiff task contributes **two** MNL observations: one best pick from the full set and one worst pick from the remaining set.\n",
        "\n",
        "An important identification issue is that only utility differences matter. If I added the same constant to every $\\beta_j$, the soft-max probabilities would not change. So one coefficient must be fixed as a reference. I normalize item 1 to have utility zero and estimate the remaining 9 coefficients."
      ],
      "id": "c1aa1821"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "rows = []\n",
        "for (rid, task), g in df.groupby([\"Record ID\", \"Task\"]):\n",
        "    g = g.sort_values(\"Position\")\n",
        "    shown_items = g[\"ItemID\"].astype(int).tolist()\n",
        "    best_item = int(g.loc[g[\"Response\"] == 1, \"ItemID\"].iloc[0])\n",
        "    worst_item = int(g.loc[g[\"Response\"] == -1, \"ItemID\"].iloc[0])\n",
        "\n",
        "    rows.append({\n",
        "        \"Record ID\": rid,\n",
        "        \"Task\": task,\n",
        "        \"shown\": shown_items,\n",
        "        \"best\": best_item,\n",
        "        \"worst\": worst_item\n",
        "    })\n",
        "\n",
        "tasks = pd.DataFrame(rows)\n",
        "tasks.head()"
      ],
      "id": "48f35c9d",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## MNL via Maximum Likelihood\n",
        "\n",
        "Let $\\beta_1 = 0$ be the reference utility, and let $\\beta_2,\\dots,\\beta_{10}$ be the free parameters. The log-likelihood over all tasks is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\ell(\\boldsymbol{\\beta}) = \\sum_{\\text{tasks}} \\left[\n",
        "\\beta_{j_b^*} - \\log \\sum_{j \\in \\text{shown}} e^{\\beta_j}\n",
        "-\\beta_{j_w^*} - \\log \\sum_{j \\in \\text{shown} \\setminus \\{j_b^*\\}} e^{-\\beta_j}\n",
        "\\right].\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The first bracketed term is the best-pick contribution, and the second is the worst-pick contribution conditional on the best pick already being removed.\n",
        "\n",
        "This likelihood is attractive because it uses more of the design structure than the counts summary does. Every item shown in a task affects the denominator of a logit probability, so every appearance contributes information. The price of that additional structure is that there is no simple closed-form estimator, so I need numerical optimization."
      ],
      "id": "104c498a"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "shown_arr = np.vstack(tasks[\"shown\"].apply(lambda x: np.array(x, dtype=int) - 1).to_numpy())\n",
        "best_idx = tasks[\"best\"].to_numpy(dtype=int) - 1\n",
        "worst_idx = tasks[\"worst\"].to_numpy(dtype=int) - 1\n",
        "\n",
        "remaining_arr = np.zeros((len(tasks), 3), dtype=int)\n",
        "for i in range(len(tasks)):\n",
        "    remaining_arr[i] = shown_arr[i][shown_arr[i] != best_idx[i]]\n",
        "\n",
        "def unpack_beta(beta_free):\n",
        "    return np.concatenate(([0.0], np.asarray(beta_free, dtype=float)))\n",
        "\n",
        "def mnl_loglike(beta_free):\n",
        "    beta = unpack_beta(beta_free)\n",
        "\n",
        "    v_shown = beta[shown_arr]\n",
        "    ll_best = beta[best_idx] - logsumexp(v_shown, axis=1)\n",
        "\n",
        "    v_remain = -beta[remaining_arr]\n",
        "    ll_worst = -beta[worst_idx] - logsumexp(v_remain, axis=1)\n",
        "\n",
        "    return np.sum(ll_best + ll_worst)\n",
        "\n",
        "def mnl_grad(beta_free):\n",
        "    beta = unpack_beta(beta_free)\n",
        "    grad_full = np.zeros(J_total)\n",
        "\n",
        "    exp_shown = np.exp(beta[shown_arr])\n",
        "    p_best = exp_shown / exp_shown.sum(axis=1, keepdims=True)\n",
        "\n",
        "    for t in range(len(tasks)):\n",
        "        grad_full[best_idx[t]] += 1.0\n",
        "        for k, item in enumerate(shown_arr[t]):\n",
        "            grad_full[item] -= p_best[t, k]\n",
        "\n",
        "    exp_worst = np.exp(-beta[remaining_arr])\n",
        "    p_worst = exp_worst / exp_worst.sum(axis=1, keepdims=True)\n",
        "\n",
        "    for t in range(len(tasks)):\n",
        "        grad_full[worst_idx[t]] -= 1.0\n",
        "        for k, item in enumerate(remaining_arr[t]):\n",
        "            grad_full[item] += p_worst[t, k]\n",
        "\n",
        "    return grad_full[1:]\n",
        "\n",
        "def neg_mnl_loglike(beta_free):\n",
        "    return -mnl_loglike(beta_free)\n",
        "\n",
        "def neg_mnl_grad(beta_free):\n",
        "    return -mnl_grad(beta_free)\n",
        "\n",
        "beta_grid = np.linspace(-2.5, 2.5, 200)\n",
        "ll_grid = []\n",
        "\n",
        "for val in beta_grid:\n",
        "    b = np.zeros(J_total - 1)\n",
        "    b[0] = val\n",
        "    ll_grid.append(mnl_loglike(b))"
      ],
      "id": "f48f930a",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: false\n",
        "plt.plot(beta_grid, ll_grid, linewidth=2)\n",
        "plt.title(\"Profile Log-Likelihood for One Utility Coefficient\")\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"Candidate value\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"Log-likelihood\")\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "0d2c58be",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The profile plot is not the full multivariate likelihood, but it is still useful. It shows the familiar shape of a well-behaved objective function: concave near the optimum and sharply worse as the parameter moves too far in either direction."
      ],
      "id": "f96243b5"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "out = minimize(\n",
        "    neg_mnl_loglike,\n",
        "    x0=np.zeros(J_total - 1),\n",
        "    jac=neg_mnl_grad,\n",
        "    method=\"BFGS\"\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "beta_hat_free = out.x\n",
        "hess_inv = out.hess_inv\n",
        "se_hat_free = np.sqrt(np.diag(hess_inv))\n",
        "\n",
        "beta_hat = unpack_beta(beta_hat_free)\n",
        "se_hat = np.concatenate(([np.nan], se_hat_free))\n",
        "shares_mle = np.exp(beta_hat) / np.exp(beta_hat).sum()\n",
        "\n",
        "mle_table = labels.copy()\n",
        "mle_table[\"beta_mle\"] = beta_hat\n",
        "mle_table[\"se_mle\"] = se_hat\n",
        "mle_table[\"share_mle\"] = shares_mle\n",
        "mle_table = mle_table.sort_values(\"share_mle\", ascending=False).reset_index(drop=True)\n",
        "\n",
        "mle_table_rounded = mle_table.copy()\n",
        "mle_table_rounded[[\"beta_mle\", \"se_mle\", \"share_mle\"]] = mle_table_rounded[[\"beta_mle\", \"se_mle\", \"share_mle\"]].round(4)\n",
        "mle_table_rounded"
      ],
      "id": "bf6ea40d",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "opt_summary = pd.DataFrame({\n",
        "    \"Quantity\": [\"Converged\", \"Function value\", \"Iterations\"],\n",
        "    \"Value\": [out.success, round(out.fun, 3), out.nit]\n",
        "})\n",
        "opt_summary"
      ],
      "id": "404fbc4d",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: false\n",
        "plot_mle = mle_table.sort_values(\"share_mle\", ascending=True)\n",
        "plt.barh(plot_mle[\"Label\"].str.replace(\"MGTA \", \"\", regex=False), plot_mle[\"share_mle\"])\n",
        "plt.title(\"MNL via Maximum Likelihood: Shares of Preference\")\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"Estimated share of preference\")\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "8bdf9b96",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The MLE estimates tell a similar story to the counts analysis, but they are now grounded in an explicit model of the best-worst choice process. The estimated shares answer a clean counterfactual question: if all 10 classes appeared on a single screen, what fraction of choices would each class receive according to the fitted MNL model?"
      ],
      "id": "be57e7b9"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "mle_summary = pd.concat([\n",
        "    mle_table_rounded[[\"Label\", \"share_mle\"]].head(3).assign(group=\"Top 3 by MLE share\"),\n",
